WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.587
In part one, the numbers sat on the pads

2
00:00:06.587 --> 00:00:07.397
heights.

3
00:00:07.397 --> 00:00:10.184
Now put numbers on the routes

4
00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:13.962
crates per hour, negative when the crates

5
00:00:14.051 --> 00:00:15.940
go against the arrow.

6
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.201
Here is a delivery job

7
00:00:18.201 --> 00:00:21.524
pad one sends out three crates an hour

8
00:00:21.524 --> 00:00:23.850
and pad five receives them.

9
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.961
How many different flows along

10
00:00:27.053 --> 00:00:29.170
the routes do this job?

11
00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.115
One, a few, or infinitely many?

12
00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:33.680
Pause and think.

13
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.053
Multiply by A transpose.

14
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.825
Entry j of A transpose y is what arrives

15
00:00:44.941 --> 00:00:48.433
at pad j minus what leaves it.

16
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:53.437
The job is a list f of net crates per pad

17
00:00:53.437 --> 00:00:57.860
minus three, zero, zero, zero, three.

18
00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.956
One flow that works

19
00:00:59.956 --> 00:01:02.400
three crates along e three

20
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.757
from pad one to pad three

21
00:01:04.757 --> 00:01:07.638
and on along e six, to pad five.

22
00:01:07.638 --> 00:01:10.955
Pad three gets three and sends three

23
00:01:10.955 --> 00:01:12.090
it balances.

24
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.079
The left nullspace holds the flows

25
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.488
with A transpose y equal to zero

26
00:01:17.559 --> 00:01:19.331
what arrives at every pad

27
00:01:19.401 --> 00:01:20.748
equals what leaves.

28
00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:22.944
One crate going round the left

29
00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:24.716
triangle is such a flow

30
00:01:24.716 --> 00:01:27.550
and so is one round the right triangle.

31
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.134
So the flow is not unique.

32
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.627
Add two of the left loop and

33
00:01:32.713 --> 00:01:35.033
take away one of the right

34
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.127
and the flow becomes two, two, one

35
00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:40.533
minus one, minus one, four.

36
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.424
It does the same job.

37
00:01:42.424 --> 00:01:45.088
The answer is infinitely many

38
00:01:45.088 --> 00:01:47.580
one flow plus any loop flow.

39
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.843
Which jobs are possible at all?

40
00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:53.568
Every row of A adds to zero

41
00:01:53.568 --> 00:01:57.515
so the entries of f must add to zero too

42
00:01:57.515 --> 00:02:01.650
crates are not created or lost at the pads.

43
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.014
Now count.

44
00:02:03.014 --> 00:02:04.425
The rank is four

45
00:02:04.503 --> 00:02:07.246
so the left nullspace has dimension

46
00:02:07.325 --> 00:02:10.382
six minus four: two, the two triangles.

47
00:02:10.382 --> 00:02:13.752
A loop round the whole bow tie is not new

48
00:02:13.752 --> 00:02:15.790
it is the sum of the two.

49
00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.567
For any connected network

50
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.553
the number of independent loops

51
00:02:21.647 --> 00:02:24.447
is edges minus nodes plus one.

52
00:02:24.447 --> 00:02:27.527
Remove routes e three and e six

53
00:02:27.527 --> 00:02:31.727
and the network becomes a tree: four routes

54
00:02:31.727 --> 00:02:32.660
no loops.

55
00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.913
Put them back and add a route e seven

56
00:02:36.913 --> 00:02:39.584
from pad two to pad four.

57
00:02:39.584 --> 00:02:42.357
Now there are three loops

58
00:02:42.357 --> 00:02:45.130
seven minus five plus one.

59
00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.512
Now multiply in the other order

60
00:02:48.512 --> 00:02:50.351
A transpose times A.

61
00:02:50.438 --> 00:02:52.802
This five by five matrix is

62
00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.816
the graph Laplacian L.

63
00:02:54.904 --> 00:02:57.443
Its diagonal holds the degree

64
00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.281
of each landing pad

65
00:02:59.281 --> 00:03:02.871
the number of arrows that touch it: two

66
00:03:02.871 --> 00:03:04.710
two, four, two, two.

67
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.671
Off the diagonal it has minus one for every

68
00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:11.779
two landing pads joined by an arrow.

69
00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:14.214
The arrows have disappeared

70
00:03:14.214 --> 00:03:17.909
L only knows which landing pads are joined.

71
00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:21.688
Every row adds to zero, so L sends one, one

72
00:03:21.688 --> 00:03:23.620
one, one, one to zero.

73
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.580
The key fact: x transpose L x is the sum

74
00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:29.655
of the squared climbs.

75
00:03:29.655 --> 00:03:32.272
For the heights of part one

76
00:03:32.272 --> 00:03:36.153
the squared climbs are sixteen, one, nine

77
00:03:36.153 --> 00:03:39.221
forty nine, four and twenty five

78
00:03:39.221 --> 00:03:42.650
and they add to one hundred and four.

79
00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.164
In machine learning

80
00:03:45.261 --> 00:03:47.581
a value on every node is

81
00:03:47.678 --> 00:03:49.612
a signal on a graph

82
00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:52.416
and x transpose L x measures

83
00:03:52.513 --> 00:03:54.060
how rough it is.

84
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.928
Say the pads have noise sensors: forty

85
00:03:57.928 --> 00:04:02.279
forty two, forty one, sixty and forty three.

86
00:04:02.279 --> 00:04:04.310
Pad four reads high.

87
00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.779
One smoothing step subtracts

88
00:04:06.855 --> 00:04:08.671
a small multiple of L x

89
00:04:08.746 --> 00:04:10.941
which pulls every pad towards

90
00:04:11.016 --> 00:04:12.152
its neighbours.

91
00:04:12.152 --> 00:04:15.254
Pad four drops to fifty two point eight

92
00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:18.357
pad three rises to forty five point two

93
00:04:18.432 --> 00:04:21.762
and the roughness falls from six hundred and

94
00:04:21.837 --> 00:04:24.940
sixty to about one hundred and forty one.

95
00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:29.066
The total stays two hundred and twenty six

96
00:04:29.066 --> 00:04:32.490
because L sends the constants to zero.

97
00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.923
So: A transpose y is arrivals minus

98
00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:39.564
departures at each pad

99
00:04:39.564 --> 00:04:43.747
and the balanced flows are the loops

100
00:04:43.747 --> 00:04:47.710
edges minus nodes plus one of them.

101
00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.590
The Laplacian, A transpose A

102
00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.121
is degrees minus neighbours

103
00:04:53.208 --> 00:04:56.611
and x transpose L x adds up the squared

104
00:04:56.698 --> 00:04:59.229
differences along the routes.

105
00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:00.188
Your turn

106
00:05:00.188 --> 00:05:03.853
send two crates from pad two to pad four

107
00:05:03.853 --> 00:05:05.860
in two different ways.
